projects=[
{'id':'ai-study-assistant','name':'AI Study Assistant','symbol':'AI','kind':'STUDY','repo':'ai-study-assistant','desc':'Turn PDF study material into a conversation. A full-stack application with document-based chat, summaries, quizzes and flashcards.','stack':['Python / FastAPI','Next.js','ChromaDB','Gemini'],'title':'From PDF to a grounded answer','note':'How document extraction, vector retrieval and Gemini connect in AI Study Assistant.','category':'ai','flow':'PDF upload → Text chunks → Embeddings → ChromaDB → Retrieved context → Gemini','sections':[
('The problem','Study material often lives in long PDFs. AI Study Assistant brings document uploads, chat and revision tools into one application, so students can work with their own material.'),
('How the system fits together','A Next.js / React frontend talks to a Python FastAPI backend. PostgreSQL and SQLAlchemy support application data, while JWT authentication identifies users. PDF text is extracted and split into chunks; Sentence Transformers creates embeddings used by ChromaDB for retrieval.'),
('The retrieval path','A question is used to retrieve relevant document chunks. Those chunks provide context for Gemini to generate an answer. Retrieval narrows the information presented to the model; it does not guarantee that every answer is correct.'),
('Study tools and boundaries','The implementation includes Gemini-based quiz generation, summaries with basic or fallback paths, and sentence-based flashcards. The inspected upload path rebuilds the ChromaDB collection, so this should not be described as a persistent multi-document knowledge base.'),
('What this project demonstrates','The project connects authentication, file processing, database persistence, a web interface and an external model API. The engineering focus is the path from an uploaded document to context-aware interaction, rather than a model trained from scratch.') ]},
{'id':'expense-tracker','name':'Expense Tracker API','symbol':'{ }','kind':'BACKEND','repo':'expense-tracker-api','desc':'A Java backend for personal expense management, with authenticated access, user-scoped records, validation and flexible filtering.','stack':['Java 21','Spring Boot','PostgreSQL','JWT'],'title':'Keeping expense records user-scoped','note':'Authentication, persistence and query design in a Spring Boot REST API.','category':'backend','flow':'HTTP request → JWT / Spring Security → Controller → Service → JPA → PostgreSQL','sections':[
('The problem','Expense records belong to individual users. A useful expense API needs more than create, read, update and delete endpoints: it also needs authentication, ownership checks and predictable input handling.'),
('Authentication and ownership','The Java 21 / Spring Boot application uses Spring Security and JWT authentication. Passwords are hashed with BCrypt. Expense operations are scoped to the authenticated user, keeping ownership relevant when records are read or changed.'),
('A layered API','Controllers handle HTTP requests and validated data transfer objects. Services contain application operations, and Spring Data JPA / Hibernate handles persistence in PostgreSQL. Global exception handling gives API failures a consistent response path.'),
('Finding the right records','The implementation supports pagination and sorting alongside category, amount and date filtering. These query capabilities make a growing expense history usable without returning every record in a single response.'),
('Testing and running locally','The repository contains JUnit and Mockito service tests, MockMvc authentication tests, and Docker / Docker Compose configuration. GitHub Actions runs a Java 21 build and test workflow with a PostgreSQL 16 service on pushes and pull requests to main. This describes the configured workflow, not a claim that its latest run passed.') ]},
{'id':'afterbell','name':'AfterBell','symbol':'AB','kind':'JOB FINDER','repo':'job_finder','desc':'A student job-finding platform with separate student, employer and administrator workflows, built with React, Flask and Supabase.','stack':['React / TypeScript','Flask','Supabase','AI integration'],'title':'One platform, three different workflows','note':'How the AfterBell frontend and backend support students, employers and administrators.','category':'full-stack','flow':'React / TypeScript → Flask API → Supabase Auth + Database → Role-specific workflows','sections':[
('The problem','Students need to discover opportunities and manage applications. Employers need to post roles and review applicants. Administrators need tools for verification and user management. AfterBell brings these related workflows into a single platform.'),
('Frontend and backend','The repository contains a React / TypeScript frontend built with Vite and a Python Flask backend. Supabase provides authentication and database services. The frontend handles the user interface while the backend exposes the application API.'),
('Role-specific workflows','Student features include job browsing, saved jobs and applications. Employer views support job posting and applicant management. Administrator views include user management and employer verification. These are repository-supported features, not a claim that every deployed workflow has been exercised.'),
('AI-assisted extraction','The backend includes extraction of job information from images, using Groq with a Gemini fallback. This supports the job-information workflow; the portfolio does not describe it as an automated hiring or candidate-ranking system.'),
('Scope of the evidence','The source structure and implementation were reviewed. A publicly hosted frontend is referenced in the README, but authenticated production workflows have not been verified, so the repository is the primary link here.') ]}
]
